    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §353, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S2410\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act\nto amend the education law, in relation to the membership of the board\nof trustees of the state university of New York\n\nPURPOSE:\nThis bill adds community college faculty representation to the State\nUniversity Board of Trustees.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\nThis bill amends Subdivision 1 of Section 353 of the Education Law,\nadding an 18th member of the State University Board of Trustees. The\nboard currently consists of 17 members (15 appointed by the Governor\nwith the advice and consent of the Senate, and the president of the\nStudent Assembly serving ex officio and the president of the\nUniversity Faculty Senate serving as a non-voting ex-officio member).\nThis legislation will give the president of the Faculty Council of\nCommunity Colleges a seat on the board, as a non-voting, ex-officio\nmember.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nThe Faculty Council of Community Colleges represents approximately\n14,000 faculty members who educate over half the students enrolled in\nSUNY and instruct the majority of its freshman and sophomore\nstudents. In 2006, the legislature affirmed the need for faculty\nrepresentation on the Board of Trustees by adding an additional seat\nfor the President of the University Faculty Senate.\nHowever, the community college faculty are not represented by the\nFaculty Senate, but are instead represented by the Faculty Council of\nCommunity Colleges. As more and more students enroll in community\ncolleges across the state, it is important for the community colleges\nto have representation on the SUNY board.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n2009-10 S.1656; 2007-08, S.3818.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nNone.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n\n",
